  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="hw03-P5-4-3">
      <title>Problem 5.4.3: <m>k</m>-fold Cross-Validation</title>

      <task xml:id="hw03-P5-4-3-a" workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Explain how <m>k</m>-fold cross-validation is implemented.
            No code is necessary; you may use words, pictures, and/or pseudocode
            to demonstrate your understanding.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw03-P5-4-3-b-i" workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What are the advantages and disadvantages of <m>k</m>-fold
            cross-validation relative to the validation set approach?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw03-P5-4-3-b-ii" workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What are the advantages and disadvantages of <m>k</m>-fold
            cross-validation relative to leave-one-out cross-validation (LOOCV)?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="hw03-P5-4-5">
      <title>Problem 5.4.5: Validation-Set Error</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          In Chapter 4, we used logistic regression to predict the probability
          of default using income and balance in the <c>Default</c> data set.
          We will now estimate the test error of this model using the validation
          set approach.
          Set a random seed before beginning your analysis.
        </p>

        <program language="python">
          <preamble visible="true">
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
          </preamble>
          <code>
url = "https://msu-cmse-courses.github.io/CMSE381-S26/_downloads/0bf0b0b65f603971cd33a04ad934449c/Default.csv"
Default = pd.read_csv(url)
Default.head()
          </code>
        </program>
      </introduction>

      <task xml:id="hw03-P5-4-5-a">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Fit a logistic regression model that uses <c>income</c> and
            <c>balance</c> to predict <c>default</c>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw03-P5-4-5-b-i">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Split the observations into a training set and a validation set.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw03-P5-4-5-b-ii">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Fit a multiple logistic regression model using only the training
            observations.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw03-P5-4-5-b-iii">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Compute the posterior probability of default for each observation in
            the validation set.
            Classify an observation as <c>Yes</c> when the probability is
            greater than 0.5 and as <c>No</c> otherwise.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw03-P5-4-5-b-iv" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Compute the validation set error: the fraction of validation
            observations that are misclassified.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw03-P5-4-5-c">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Repeat the process in part (b) three times using three different
            splits into training and validation sets.
            Comment on the results.
            Do not regenerate the original data; changing the split (for
            example, by changing the random seed) is sufficient.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw03-P5-4-5-d">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Now use <c>income</c>, <c>balance</c>, and a dummy variable for
            <c>student</c> to predict default.
            Estimate the test error using the validation set approach, and
            comment on whether including the student dummy variable reduces the
            test error rate.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="hw03-P5-4-8">
      <title>Problem 5.4.8: Cross-Validation on Simulated Data</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          Generate the simulated data below.
        </p>

        <program language="python">
          <code>
rng = np.random.default_rng(1)
x = rng.normal(size=100)
y = x - 2 * x**2 + rng.normal(size=100)
          </code>
        </program>
      </introduction>

      <task xml:id="hw03-P5-4-8-a" workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            In this data set, what are <m>n</m> and <m>p</m>?
            Write the model used to generate the data in equation form.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw03-P5-4-8-b">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Create a scatterplot of <m>X</m> against <m>Y</m> and comment on
            what you find.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw03-P5-4-8-c">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Set a random seed and compute the LOOCV errors from least-squares
            fits of polynomial models of degrees 1, 2, 3, and 4.
            Plot LOOCV error against polynomial degree.
          </p>

          <p>
            The four models are:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>Y = \beta_0 + \beta_1X + \varepsilon</m>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>Y = \beta_0 + \beta_1X + \beta_2X^2 + \varepsilon</m>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>Y = \beta_0 + \beta_1X + \beta_2X^2 + \beta_3X^3 + \varepsilon</m>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>Y = \beta_0 + \beta_1X + \beta_2X^2 + \beta_3X^3 + \beta_4X^4 + \varepsilon</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw03-P5-4-8-d" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Repeat part (c) using another random seed and report your results.
            Are the results the same?
            Explain why or why not.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw03-P5-4-8-e" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Which model in part (c) has the smallest LOOCV error?
            Is this what you expected?
            Explain.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw03-P5-4-8-f">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Repeat part (c) using <m>k</m>-fold cross-validation for
            <m>k=5,10,15,20</m>.
            On one figure, plot test error against degree for all four values of
            <m>k</m> and for LOOCV.
            What do you notice?
            You do not need to plot training error.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="hw03-subset-selection">
      <title>Forward and Backward Selection</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          The table reports training and test mean squared errors from fitting
          linear regressions to predict <c>mpg</c> using different subsets of
          variables from the <c>Auto</c> data set.
        </p>

        <table>
          <title>Training and test error for candidate models</title>

          <tabular>
            <row header="yes">
              <cell>Variables</cell>
              <cell>Train error</cell>
              <cell>Test error</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Null model</cell>
              <cell>60.76</cell>
              <cell>60.73</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell><c>cylinders</c></cell>
              <cell>24.02</cell>
              <cell>24.15</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell><c>horsepower</c></cell>
              <cell>23.94</cell>
              <cell>24.19</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell><c>weight</c></cell>
              <cell>18.68</cell>
              <cell>18.84</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell><c>acceleration</c></cell>
              <cell>49.87</cell>
              <cell>50.26</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell><c>cylinders</c>, <c>horsepower</c></cell>
              <cell>20.85</cell>
              <cell>21.13</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell><c>cylinders</c>, <c>weight</c></cell>
              <cell>18.38</cell>
              <cell>18.55</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell><c>cylinders</c>, <c>acceleration</c></cell>
              <cell>23.94</cell>
              <cell>24.38</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell><c>horsepower</c>, <c>weight</c></cell>
              <cell>17.84</cell>
              <cell>18.03</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell><c>horsepower</c>, <c>acceleration</c></cell>
              <cell>22.46</cell>
              <cell>22.70</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell><c>weight</c>, <c>acceleration</c></cell>
              <cell>18.25</cell>
              <cell>18.61</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell><c>cylinders</c>, <c>horsepower</c>, <c>weight</c></cell>
              <cell>17.76</cell>
              <cell>17.99</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <c>cylinders</c>, <c>horsepower</c>, <c>acceleration</c>
              </cell>
              <cell>20.06</cell>
              <cell>20.44</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell><c>cylinders</c>, <c>weight</c>, <c>acceleration</c></cell>
              <cell>18.13</cell>
              <cell>18.54</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell><c>horsepower</c>, <c>weight</c>, <c>acceleration</c></cell>
              <cell>17.84</cell>
              <cell>18.16</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>All four predictors</cell>
              <cell>17.76</cell>
              <cell>18.13</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
          For parts (a), (b), and (c), describe the algorithm, identify
          <m>M_k</m> for <m>k=0,1,\ldots,4</m>, state the best model returned,
          and report how many models must be trained.
          No code is required.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task xml:id="hw03-subset-a" workspace="2in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Apply best subset selection.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw03-subset-b" workspace="2in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Apply forward stepwise selection.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw03-subset-c" workspace="2in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Apply backward stepwise selection.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw03-subset-d" workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Are your answers to parts (a), (b), and (c) the same?
            Should we generally expect them to be the same?
            Explain.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>
